PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The consensus on Part (1) remains firmly established as "Yes, No, No" across all participants. The ongoing disagreement between Choice O (Special trading rules and regulations) and Choice Q (Market inefficiency) continues to center on whether the 15-second post-trade drift is driven by microstructural liquidity frictions or regulatory constraints. Choice O remains the structurally sound answer because generic market inefficiency and slow quote adjustment would mathematically produce symmetric drift for both buys and sells under the prompt's sign-adjusted metric. Mainland China's unique institutional rules—specifically the T+1 trading lock-up and severe short-selling constraints—are required to generate the directional asymmetry by concentrating high conviction in aggressive buys while sells remain primarily liquidity-driven. Order-book resilience merely mediates the short-term absorption of this regulatory-driven flow, making Choice O the definitive explanation.
}

